Z||000003||87GTZ34||05-04-2001||10:55 AM||||Ken I have that one also and the book does list it (a vacuum canister).  The other one I'm talking about is on the passenger side built into the side of the car.  ie it is not visible from anywhere but underneath.  It does look like another gas vapor canister but why???||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||05-04-2001||11:32 AM||||fuel vapor canister. Supposed to keep gasoline fumes from venting into the air. but who knows, I can still smell gas. pass side on mine didnt have one, i had whole body off.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 05-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||skitime||05-04-2001||11:39 AM||||It is a vacuum reservoir for the cruise control.  Non cruise control model do not have them.||||reg||1
Z||000006||87GTZ34||05-04-2001||01:05 PM||||Okay let me clarify if I can... I have the fuel vapor purge canister which is located on the driver side just behind the intake.  It is black & plaastic and has vacuum lines hooked up to it.  <P>I also have the vacuum accumulator which looks like a black coffee can and is mounted on the back of the eng bay along with the cruise diaphram (can I say that word on a public forum)<P>The can that I'm talking about is on the right side between the structural wall next to the original battery location and the outer plastic.  From underneath the car I see two steel lines coming from it.  It does look like it belongs to the fuel vapor or overflow system but I haven't traced it out yet.  The '87 GM manual says nothing about it in any section.  Next time you are under your car with room to move around, look there and see if has this tank.||||reg||1
